# Reproduces the toolchain on a new machine.
# Applied automatically by run_onchange_before_install-packages.sh.tmpl,
# which re-runs whenever this file changes.

# `brew bundle` is built into Homebrew now; no tap needed.

# ── Terminal ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
cask "ghostty"
cask "font-jetbrains-mono-nerd-font"
brew "tmux"
brew "starship"
brew "sesh"
# zsh-autosuggestions and zsh-syntax-highlighting are NOT here. .zshrc lists
# them in oh-my-zsh's `plugins` array, so omz loads its own clones from
# ~/.oh-my-zsh/custom/plugins (created by the installer) and the Homebrew copies
# sat installed and never sourced. One source of truth per plugin, and the one
# kept is the framework's own convention.

# ── Editors ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# This setup is terminal-first: the IDE is Neovim, installed below with the rest
# of the CLI. Cursor is the one GUI editor here, kept for its model rather than
# its editing surface. No Cursor settings are managed -- only the app.
#
# Android Studio is installed through JetBrains Toolbox, not brew -- which is why
# it lives in ~/Applications and `brew list` has never heard of it. Adding
# `cask "android-studio"` would therefore install a SECOND copy into
# /Applications, two apps sharing one set of config dirs. So Toolbox is what
# belongs here, and Android Studio comes from it.
#
# Toolbox also earns its place for a reason the Brewfile can check: ~/.zprofile
# puts Toolbox's scripts directory on PATH, which is where the `studio1` and
# `idea` launchers live. That PATH entry was managed while the thing that creates
# it was not.
#
# Xcode stays absent: not a cask at all, only the App Store or a developer.apple
# download. Both ship their own JDK and manage their own SDKs, so nothing in this
# repo depends on either.
cask "cursor"
cask "jetbrains-toolbox"

# ── Core CLI ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
brew "neovim"
brew "chezmoi"
brew "git"
brew "git-delta"
brew "lazygit"
brew "gh"
brew "ripgrep"
brew "fd"
brew "fzf"
brew "bat"
brew "eza"
brew "zoxide"
brew "atuin"
brew "jq"
brew "btop"
brew "direnv"
brew "tlrc"
brew "hexyl"
brew "duf"
brew "dust"
brew "procs"
brew "sd"
brew "hyperfine"

# BOTH tree-sitter formulae are needed and neither is redundant. `tree-sitter` is
# the library; `tree-sitter-cli` is the `tree-sitter` binary. nvim-treesitter is
# pinned to its `main` branch (see lazy-lock.json), which compiles parsers by
# shelling out to that binary -- without it, parsers fail to build and the only
# symptom is missing highlighting. This machine had the CLI installed by hand and
# the Brewfile never captured it, so a fresh Mac silently lost treesitter.
brew "tree-sitter"
brew "tree-sitter-cli"

# fontconfig is listed on purpose even though it arrives anyway as a dependency
# of apktool, jadx and friends. `fc-list` is what bootstrap.sh uses to prove a
# Nerd Font glyph actually exists in the installed font -- the one check that
# catches the failure mode where a stale codepoint renders as nothing rather than
# tofu. That check is wrapped in `command -v fc-list`, so without an explicit
# entry, trimming the security section would make it silently disappear instead
# of fail.
brew "fontconfig"

# Terminal browser. chawan lays pages out with real CSS and draws text as
# text, so it stays sharp; it replaced browsh, which rasterised the page into
# half-block characters and looked pixelated regardless of terminal.
brew "chawan"

# yazi -- terminal file manager. The gap it fills: file browsing otherwise only
# exists inside Neovim (neo-tree), with nothing for navigating and previewing
# outside the editor. No config needed; the `y` cd-on-exit wrapper from upstream
# docs is deliberately NOT added, to keep hand-written shell minimal.
brew "yazi"

# ── Runtimes ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# mise owns node, java and ruby -- see ~/.config/mise/config.toml, the single
# place those versions are pinned. Flutter is NOT here: it is pinned per-repo
# by FVM, installed via `dart pub global activate fvm` or the fvm installer.
brew "mise"
brew "luarocks"

# ruby-build without rbenv: mise's ruby backend uses it to build interpreters.
# rbenv itself is gone -- mise pins ruby in ~/.config/mise/config.toml and
# provides the shims, which is what rbenv was here for.
brew "ruby-build"

# ── Mobile / build ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
brew "cocoapods"
brew "fastlane"
brew "xcbeautify"
brew "xcode-build-server"
brew "swiftformat"
brew "swiftlint"
# ── Device mirroring ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
brew "scrcpy"

# The container/Kubernetes toolchain is NOT here. It is roughly a gigabyte and
# only earns that on a machine doing backend work, so it lives in
# `Brewfile.optional` and is installed by hand with `brewopt`. This is not the
# work/personal split coming back: nothing branches at apply time, every machine
# still gets an identical baseline, and the extra group is a command a human runs
# once. Dropping it from this file does not uninstall anything -- `brew bundle`
# only ever installs -- so a machine that already has Docker keeps it.

# ── Security ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# For applications you are authorized to test.
brew "semgrep"
brew "gitleaks"
# trufflehog, grype, dex2jar and ffuf were here and are now in the `secextra`
# opt-in group: each duplicates a tool the config actually calls (gitleaks,
# trivy, jadx) or targets web pentesting this baseline does not wire up.
brew "trivy"
brew "osv-scanner"
brew "syft"
brew "jadx"
brew "apktool"
brew "nmap"
brew "nuclei"
brew "jwt-cli"
cask "mitmproxy"   # ships as a cask, not a formula
# uv, not pipx, for the two Python security tools. pipx installed them against
# Homebrew's python, so a `brew upgrade python` orphaned the venvs and broke
# frida-ps/objection with an error pointing nowhere near the cause. uv manages an
# interpreter per tool, so brew cannot reach them.
brew "uv"

# ── Architecture ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# C4 models as code. `structurizr` is the unified binary -- `local` serves the
# browser UI (it replaces the end-of-life Structurizr Lite), `export` writes
# Mermaid, and `validate`/`inspect` are the only diagnostics that exist for the
# DSL. Driven by ~/.config/zsh/c4.zsh.
#
# NOT `structurizr-cli`: that formula is deprecated upstream and Homebrew
# disables it on 2027-02-17. This is its named replacement. Do not "fix" this
# back.
#
# No container runtime is needed. Every guide still says to run Structurizr Lite
# under Docker; the native binary makes that unnecessary, which is why colima and
# docker stayed in Brewfile.optional where they were.
#
# It depends on Homebrew's openjdk, already installed here as a dependency of
# jadx and apktool. That dependency is load-bearing, not incidental: the war is
# built for Java 21+, and Homebrew's launcher script does
#
#     export JAVA_HOME="${JAVA_HOME:-<brew openjdk>}"
#
# which is a FALLBACK, not a pin. ~/.zshenv exports mise's temurin-17 for Gradle
# and jdtls, so out of the box every structurizr call inherits Java 17 and dies
# with UnsupportedClassVersionError. `_c4_structurizr` in ~/.config/zsh/c4.zsh
# overrides JAVA_HOME for this command only -- do not "simplify" that away, and
# do not change the global JAVA_HOME to fix it, because the Android toolchain
# needs 17 exactly.
brew "structurizr"
